<DocsLayout
  title="Modes — Apple Docs"
  description="Plan, Agent and Super Agent: what each Apple mode does, what it costs in Credits, and how to choose the right one for the job."
  heading="Modes: Plan, Agent, Super Agent"
>
  <p>
    Every message you send wakes one of three apples. They differ in how much autonomy they take
    and how much work they do per request — and therefore in what they cost. The craft of using
    Apple well is mostly picking the smallest apple that can carry the job.
  </p>

  <h2>Plan — 2 credits</h2>
  <p><em>Fast answers and small edits.</em></p>
  <p>
    Plan is conversational and quick. It reads the parts of your project that matter to your
    question and responds in seconds. Use Plan for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“What does this script do?” / “Why is this deprecated?”</li>
    <li>Single-script edits: tweak a value, rename things, fix an obvious bug.</li>
    <li>Roblox API questions with your project as context.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    At 2 credits per request — about thirty a day on the free plan — Plan is the cheapest
    way to work, and the cheapest way to <em>design</em> a big build before handing the
    design to a bigger apple.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent — 4 credits</h2>
  <p><em>Builds features across your project.</em></p>
  <p>
    Agent is the everyday builder. It reads your project tree, edits multiple scripts, creates
    and wires instances, and reports what it did. Use Agent for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Add a shop that sells speed boosts for coins.”</li>
    <li>“Make a checkpoint system for the obby.”</li>
    <li>Refactors that touch several scripts at once.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Agent finishes a request in one pass and then stops — it does not playtest on its own. Press
    Play, see what you think, and tell it what to change.
  </p>

  <h2>Super Agent — 10 credits, +1 per extra step</h2>
  <p><em>Plans, builds, tests and fixes autonomously.</em></p>
  <p>
    Super Agent is the deep agent. Give it an outcome, and it writes a plan, executes it step by step,
    runs your game in Studio, reads the output, and repairs what breaks — repeating until the
    outcome holds or it needs your input. Use Super Agent for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Build a complete round system: intermission, map vote, teleport, win detection.”</li>
    <li>“Something is breaking coins on death — find it and fix it, prove it with a run.”</li>
    <li>Anything where “done” means <em>verified working</em>, not <em>code written</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Super Agent requests start at 10 credits and add <strong>1 credit per extra step</strong> beyond the
    included ones. The running cost is displayed live during the run, and you can stop a Super Agent run
    at any moment — completed steps stay applied (and undoable).
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--violet">
    <p>
      <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> question → Plan. Feature → Agent. Outcome you want proven →
      Super Agent. When unsure, start one size smaller; escalating costs you nothing but the retry.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Switching modes</h2>
  <p>
    The mode picker sits next to the message box. Every message can use a different mode — a
    common rhythm is Plan to plan, Super Agent to build, Plan to ask “what did you just do?”, Agent to
    polish. All modes share the same project memory, so nothing is lost between switches.
  </p>

  <h2>Costs at a glance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plan</strong> — 2 credits per request</li>
    <li><strong>Agent</strong> — 4 credits per request</li>
    <li><strong>Super Agent</strong> — 10 credits per request, +1 per extra step</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/credits-and-limits">Credits &amp; limits</a> for how the daily quota works.</p>
</DocsLayout>
